<KnowledgeLayout title="How to Calculate PDF, DOCX, Code & Image Tokens" description="A technical workflow for calculating tokens from PDFs, DOCX documents, source code, data files, images, and pasted text before an LLM API call." heading="How to calculate tokens in PDFs, DOCX, code, and images" eyebrow="Multimodal workflow · Engineering guide" datePublished="2026-08-28" dateModified="2026-08-28" section="Token engineering" keywords={['PDF token calculator','DOCX token calculator','image tokens','code tokens','LLM cost']} {toc}>
  <p class="knowledge-lead">A production LLM request is rarely one text box. It can contain a confidential instruction, extracted report pages, proprietary repository fragments, structured data, and one or more images. Measuring only the pasted prompt systematically understates both context occupancy and input cost; uploading the rest to a separate counter can create an unnecessary enterprise disclosure path.</p>
  <div class="technical-callout"><span>Combined input equation</span><code>T<sub>request</sub> = T<sub>prompt</sub> + ΣT<sub>extracted files</sub> + ΣT<sub>vision</sub></code></div>
  <h2 id="pipeline">The multimodal measurement pipeline</h2>
  <ol><li><strong>Acquire locally.</strong> The browser receives the selected files; 100% of submitted prompt, document, code, data, and image content remains in the active browser session.</li><li><strong>Classify by modality.</strong> Plain text and source formats are decoded directly, rich documents enter an extraction path, and images enter a dimension/formula path.</li><li><strong>Normalize text.</strong> Extracted strings are combined with pasted input without pretending that document bytes equal model tokens.</li><li><strong>Apply the model method.</strong> OpenAI-compatible text uses the configured BPE vocabulary; other families use a disclosed deterministic projection. Images use supported provider formulas.</li><li><strong>Aggregate in a Web Worker.</strong> The calculation runs off the main interface thread without a token-counting API request.</li><li><strong>Project context and cost.</strong> Input tokens are compared with the model context limit and multiplied by the selected model's input price.</li></ol>
  <h2 id="documents">PDF and DOCX are extraction problems first</h2>
  <p>A PDF stores positioned drawing instructions, fonts, and sometimes raster scans—not necessarily a clean reading-order string. A DOCX is a ZIP-based package of XML parts. TokenCalculator.dev extracts readable PDF text and DOCX paragraph content locally, then tokenizes the resulting text. A scanned PDF without a text layer needs OCR and is deliberately reported as unsupported rather than assigned a misleading count.</p>
  <p>Extraction order can differ from the order a provider's own file-ingestion system reconstructs. Headers, footers, tables, hidden runs, OCR, and provider-added document framing are the main reconciliation points.</p>
  <h2 id="code">Source code and data files preserve expensive syntax</h2>
  <p>JSON keys, escaped strings, indentation, punctuation, UUIDs, minified bundles, and base64-like sequences can tokenize very differently from natural language. The local pipeline decodes supported source and data formats as text; it does not summarize them before counting. That makes the result useful for repository review, log analysis, RAG chunk planning, and batch prompts.</p>
  <h2 id="images">Images are geometry, detail, and model generation</h2>
  <p>Image file size is not a safe token proxy. Providers resize, tile, patch, or cap images according to model-specific rules. The browser reads dimensions locally and applies the documented formula supported by the selected model. See the <a href="/blog/image-token-calculation-openai-claude-gemini/">provider image-token analysis</a> and <a href="/docs/images/">image reference</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="validation">Validate the result before budgeting production</h2>
  <p>Use the local count as a preflight measurement. For material spend, compare it with the provider's token-count endpoint or response usage metadata using the same model, message structure, tool definitions, image detail, and files. The difference is useful telemetry: it reveals provider framing and preprocessing that no browser-only calculator can observe.</p>
